Build a \href{https://github.com/marbl/Krona}{Krona}-style interactive taxonomy
explorer from a \link[phyloseq:phyloseq-class]{phyloseq::phyloseq} object, \strong{without} needing
KronaTools installed. The interactive version (default) is a D3.js
\code{htmlwidget} that renders in the RStudio viewer, in Shiny, and in R
Markdown documents, and can be saved as a self-contained \code{.html} file via
\code{file_path}. A static ggplot2 version is also available
(\code{interactive = FALSE}) for publication-ready output.

Two layouts are supported:
\itemize{
\item \code{"sunburst"} (default): concentric rings, one per taxonomic rank; the
\strong{angle} of each wedge is proportional to its value. This is the Krona
pie. Click a wedge to zoom into that subtree (Krona's signature
interaction).
\item \code{"treemap"}: nested rectangles; the \strong{area} of each rectangle is
proportional to its value. Click a cell to zoom in.
}

Colours follow the Krona convention: each distinct value at the
\code{color_by} rank receives its own hue, and descendants inherit the parent
hue with a progressive lightness shift (darker as you go deeper).

This function is a drop-in alternative to \code{\link[MiscMetabar:krona]{MiscMetabar::krona()}}, which
shells out to KronaTools and does not work on Windows. \code{krona_like_pq()}
works on all platforms because it bundles D3.js locally.
}
\examples{
\donttest{
data(data_fungi_mini, package = "MiscMetabar")

# Static sunburst (no extra dependency needed)
krona_like_pq(data_fungi_mini, interactive = FALSE)

# Static treemap
krona_like_pq(data_fungi_mini, layout = "treemap", interactive = FALSE)

# Weight by ASV count instead of read count
